# ⚠ **LF in the working tree, on every platform, and it is this repository's own declaration.**
# `.editorconfig` line 5 says `end_of_line = lf` for the whole tree. Git did not know that, so on
# Windows — where `core.autocrlf=true` is Git for Windows' default and what the hosted runner image
# ships — a checkout rewrote every file this line now covers into CRLF, and the tree the tests ran
# against was not the tree that was committed.
#
# ⚠ The exemptions below were each added after a red Windows leg or by reasoning from one, and
# every one of them is a *narrower* answer to this same question. They were never wrong; they were
# just never generalised, so the next file to be compared byte for byte was unprotected again — and
# three of them were, all at once:
#
#   1. **Thirteen formatter tests.** They express their input as a C# raw string literal, which
#      takes its line endings from the .cs file on disk. Under a CRLF checkout the input carried
#      `\r\n`, the formatter faithfully preserved it, and the expected value — written with
#      explicit `\n` in a normal literal — did not match. `TriviaTests`, `IndentationTests`,
#      `BraceTests`, `BreakPositionTests` and `FormatterTagTests`, red on Windows and nowhere else.
#   2. **`RuleCatalogTests.TheCoverageBlock_MatchesTheRegistry`.** `skala rules docs` writes the
#      generated block with `\n`; the block it was compared against had been CRLF'd on the way out.
#   3. **`CanonicalCommandTests.Canonical_RegeneratesThePayloadIdentically`.** Exactly the defect
#      the `editor_config_template` comment below describes, one file over: `canonical.editorconfig`
#      was `-text` and `canonical.json`, which the same assertion compares, was not.
#
# `text=auto` leaves anything git detects as binary alone. Measured before it was set: the only
# files in this repository stored with a CR are the fifteen under `Testing/corpus/`, and that
# directory is exempted on the next line, so nothing here normalises a byte anybody wanted.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ⚠ The corpus is data, not source. Several files exist precisely to carry CRLF, mixed endings or
# a BOM, and git's end-of-line normalisation would quietly delete the thing they test.
Testing/corpus/** -text

# The Rider export and the repository's copy of it are compared byte for byte by `config diff`.
editor_config_template -text

# ⚠ …and so is the repository's own `.editorconfig`, which is the line above's file with
# `root = true` prepended (ADR-015) — `EditorConfigIngestionTests` asserts exactly that with
# `Assert.EndsWith(template, own)`. Only one of the two was `-text`, so a Windows checkout under
# git's default `core.autocrlf=true` CRLF'd this one and left the export alone, and the assertion
# failed on Windows and nowhere else. Found by adding the cross-platform matrix, which is what a
# matrix is for.
.editorconfig -text
Distribution/Rikarin.Skala.Canonical/canonical.editorconfig -text

# The fixtures are inputs, on the same argument as the corpus: a rule fixture is a file in the
# exact shape a rule fires on, and an ingestion fixture is another repository's real file. Both are
# measured rather than compiled, and normalising their bytes changes the measurement.
Core/Rikarin.Skala.Core.Tests/Fixtures/** -text
Rules/Rikarin.Skala.Rules.Tests/fixtures/** -text

# ⚠ `docs/site/` is generated output compared byte for byte — exactly the shape of the
# `.editorconfig` defect above, and found by reasoning from it rather than by a red Windows leg.
# `skala docs site` writes `\n` on every platform (DocsSite's remarks say why at length, and
# `DocsSiteTests.NoPage_CarriesACarriageReturn` asserts it), so a Windows checkout under git's
# default `core.autocrlf=true` would CRLF all 143 files on the way out and
# `Site_IsUpToDateWithTheSources` would fail on Windows and nowhere else. The generator is the
# authority on these bytes; git is not.
docs/site/** -text
